findings.body:
The base size is now taken with `malloc_usable_size((void *)o)`, but the rest of the codebase reads allocation sizes through `zmalloc_size()`. `kvobjCreate()` allocates kvobjs with `zmalloc_usable()`, so the matching accessor is `zmalloc_size()`.

The bare libc call is a problem in two ways. In `src/zmalloc.h`, `malloc_usable_size` is only declared on the libc/glibc branch, which is skipped when `USE_JEMALLOC` is set (the default Linux build). `src/object.c` does not include `malloc.h`, so on that build the call is an implicit declaration. Modern GCC and Clang reject that by default. On builds where the symbol does resolve, it reads the wrong allocator for memory that came from jemalloc or tcmalloc, and it skips the `PREFIX_SIZE` handling that `zmalloc_size()` does.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1210-1226"}

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1217-1217"}
    size_t asize = zmalloc_size(o);

The quicklist estimate was `(double)elesize/elecount*ql->count`. `elesize` is the summed cost of the sampled nodes, and `elecount` is the number of elements in those nodes, so the division gives the cost per element. Multiplying by `ql->count` (the total element count) gives the total. The PR changes the divisor to `samples`, the number of nodes sampled. The result is the cost per node times the total element count, which overestimates by roughly the number of elements per node.

For example, a list with 1000 elements spread across 100 nodes of 10 elements each, sampled at 5 nodes: the old code gives about 1000 times the per-element cost. The new code gives about 10 times that, roughly 10x too large. The `elecount` accumulator is now unused.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1234-1240"}

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1239-1239"}
            asize += (double)elesize/elecount*ql->count;

The stream branch estimates the first N-1 listpacks at the sampled average, then adds the exact size of the last listpack. The comment above the block says the same thing. The PR changes the multiplier from `numele-1` to `numele`, so the average is applied to all N listpacks, and the last listpack's exact size is then added on top. The last listpack is therefore counted twice, and the total is overstated by about one average listpack.

The `numele-1` form was correct. `numele` is the number of listpacks in the radix tree, and the code only enters this branch when `numele > samples`, so `numele-1` is always at least 1.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1326-1335"}

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1330-1330"}
            asize += lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1);
